      "recommendation": "a, because b and c are measured to lose the grant mid-approval; a is the only contract-first spelling (declared provenance instead of a convention on granted_by)."
    },
    {
      "question": "Q3 (only if A). Scope and lifetime of the grant: which slots receive it and when is it revoked? Measured: an unstaffed-at-open position slot is stored as the position literal (no user exists at route time); the slate changes at 10 write sites (decide, recall, send back, reassign, escalate, record delete, dead-run abandon).",
      "options": [
        "i — read-only, concrete user slots only, minted at open, moved on reassign/delegate/escalate, revoked when the slot leaves the slate or the request leaves pending",
        "ii — as i, plus binding position-literal slots when a holder is assigned (needs a hook on sys_user_position writes)",
        "iii — as i, but kept for approvers who acted (mirrors the 'already acted' request visibility)"
      ],
      "recommendation": "i, because it is the narrowest grant that matches the decision right; ii adds a second trigger surface for a rare shape; iii turns a transient grant into standing access."
    },
    {
      "question": "Q4 (only if A or B). Opt-in surface: does every approval confer the read, or does the approval node declare it?",
      "options": [
        "platform default for every approval node (no authoring surface; behaviour change for every private-OWD approval)",
        "node-declared key (packages/spec authoring surface) refused at publish when the target object is not under record-sharing enforcement (today's runtime inertness guard moved to publish time)"
      ],
      "recommendation": "node-declared with publish-time refusal, because a declared capability must be enforced where declared and loudly refused where it cannot be (classes A and C cannot be enforced by a share)."
    },
    {
      "question": "Q5 (holds under C, objectui side). For class A the console's readability probe answers 403 PERMISSION_DENIED, the probe fails open by design and keeps the record link, and the record page's catch-all renders 'does not exist or may have been deleted' (recordReadability.ts fail-open branch; RecordDetailView.tsx catch → missing; code read at pin, browser NOT MEASURED). Class A is the default for an approver holding only the platform member set.",
